        "name": "6–6 ½ lbs chicken breast, raw"
      }
    },
    {
      "uuid": "825CBF58-9042-568A-832E-FB5932F840B6",
      "order": 1,
      "ingredient": {
        "uuid": "97510C68-24DF-5489-99C1-7DFE5705687B",
        "name": "jasmine rice (dry)"
      },
      "quantity": {
        "quantityType": "CUP",
        "amount": 3.5
      }
    },
    {
      "uuid": "D0734C61-9BC4-5F9A-8F5C-2E8E34A22DDE",
      "order": 2,
      "ingredient": {
        "uuid": "AA47E063-22A0-596A-90E0-655F127B5656",
        "name": "3 ½–4 lbs broccoli florets"
      }
    },
    {
      "uuid": "0319567F-2BA1-5421-9E25-CFFEC7A997B5",
      "order": 3,
      "ingredient": {
        "uuid": "92CB2AF5-8E39-5C75-A657-7EAA180E80AF",
        "name": "bottles teriyaki sauce (~422 g total)"
      },
      "quantity": {
        "quantityType": "ITEM",
        "amount": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "uuid": "56FC8DE4-D0D9-5E5A-8260-6CA366C08E0D",
      "order": 4,
      "ingredient": {
        "uuid": "EE00356D-8621-51AF-B0C3-D134C075B961",
        "name": "Olive oil, salt, pepper, garlic powder"
      }
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "uuid": "E9531496-0846-5FB4-9EC7-6A9132829EDE",
      "order": 0,
      "step": "Preheat the main oven to 425°F with the sheet pans inside, and the Ninja toaster oven to 400°F air roast. Hot pans sear on contact instead of steaming.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "3E8D70B2-1313-5FFD-945F-B0B6625A8EA3",
      "order": 1,
      "step": "Rinse 3 ½ cups jasmine rice 2–3 times until the water runs mostly clear. Add to the Instant Pot with 3 ½ cups water (1:1).",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "5B960096-2965-5D43-B0A9-EC2D2C30B0C9",
      "order": 2,
      "step": "Confirm the valve is set to SEALING. Pressure Cook (Manual) on High for 3 minutes, natural release 10 minutes, then quick release. Start this first — it takes ~10 minutes to come to pressure. Fluff and leave the lid off to cool.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "8088DCE2-4CF5-5B0E-BBE5-A874DF67AAD8",
      "order": 3,
      "step": "Cube the chicken into 1-inch pieces. This is easier while the breasts are partially frozen. Trim any tough bits — that waste is why the buy quantity is 6–6 ½ lbs rather than 5.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "7F652A94-0D4B-5683-808E-70989AF42369",
      "order": 4,
      "step": "Pat the cubed chicken completely dry with paper towels. This is the single biggest factor in whether it roasts or steams.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "DC360E47-FB8E-52FC-83EA-D19A267721EC",
      "order": 5,
      "step": "Toss the chicken with olive oil, salt, pepper, and garlic powder. Keep the seasoning light — the teriyaki sauce is already salty.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "75C246E1-0F6A-54CB-97E3-8F6628A765D3",
      "order": 6,
      "step": "Pull the hot pans and spread the chicken across 2 pans in a single layer, nothing touching. Roast at 425°F in the main oven for 22–25 minutes, flipping halfway, until it reaches 165°F.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "E012E914-2BE3-5982-B618-7070242D3700",
      "order": 7,
      "step": "Toss the broccoli with olive oil, salt, and pepper. Air roast at 400°F in the Ninja toaster oven for 12–15 minutes until the edges are slightly crispy. This runs in parallel with the chicken; do 2 batches if it does not fit in a single layer.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "0950BDE0-8DAE-51EA-B30E-5AF2CED87B74",
      "order": 8,
      "step": "Let the chicken, rice, and broccoli cool 10–15 minutes before portioning. Spread the rice on a sheet pan to cool it faster.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "8AF3B75F-3432-5F81-BC15-93565431F892",
      "order": 9,
      "step": "Assembly line across 10 open containers: 145 g chicken, then 140 g rice, then 120 g broccoli.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "21A7845F-1B3D-5FBC-84D5-4D51919E8511",
      "order": 10,
      "step": "Portion ~42 g teriyaki sauce into 10 separate small containers. The sauce is packed separately here — adding it before storage makes the rice soggy by day 2.",
      "isSection": false
    },
    {
      "uuid": "EACCFC71-E216-5290-B727-FE9BAE280F58",
      "order": 11,
      "step": "Label with a dry-erase marker. 6 containers to the fridge, 4 to the freezer. Move freezer containers to the fridge the night before eating.",
      "isSection": false
    }
  ],
  "notes": "UNTESTED — v1, never cooked\nQuantities are planned, not observed. Weigh the components and update the recipe before trusting these numbers.\n\nPER CONTAINER (10 total)\n  chicken: 145 g\n  rice: 140 g\n  broccoli: 120 g\n  sauce: 42 g\n  total: 447 g\n\nSTORAGE: 6 fridge, 4 freezer\nFreezer containers move to the fridge the night before eating.\n\nUNTESTED IN THIS REPO — the quantities below came from earlier cook sessions\nbut no dated session record exists yet, so nothing is recorded in `yields`.\nWeigh the roasted chicken after step 7, the cooked rice after step 3, and the\nroasted broccoli after step 8, then promote to dialed-in with a real date.\n\nLessons carried over from those earlier cooks:\n\nCubed chicken roasted at 425°F yields roughly 58% of raw weight once trim\nwaste is counted, which is why the buy quantity is 6–6 ½ lbs to land 145 g\nacross 10 containers. That figure sits at the top of the 0.41–0.58 range in\nstanding parameters — worth confirming with a scale, because the bottom of\nthat range would leave this recipe about 500 g short.\n\nRice was bumped to 3 ½ cups dry to account for what sticks to the pot and to\ngive fuller portions. 3 ½–4 lbs of broccoli is plenty for 10 containers; an\nearlier version overbought badly. Two bottles of teriyaki coats everything\nwith some to spare.\n\nSodium is the problem with this recipe. At roughly 1400 mg per container it is\nby far the worst in the rotation and nearly double the 800 mg ceiling, almost\nall of it from the bottled sauce. Added sugar at ~12 g is under the ceiling\nbut not by much. This is exactly the bottled-sauce pattern standing parameters\nwarns about, and the honey garlic recipe is the worked example of the fix —\na scratch sauce there lands at 700 mg. A scratch teriyaki of soy, mirin,\nginger, garlic, and a cornstarch slurry would do the same here.\n\nSauce is packed separately in this recipe, unlike the others, where the sauce\nis already in the meat. Add it after reheating.\n\nReheat in the air fryer at 350°F for 5–7 minutes. Rice and chicken freeze\nwell; broccoli softens slightly but holds.",
  "neutritionalInfo": "Serving Size: 1 container,\nCalories: 540 kcal,\nProtein: 52 g,\nCarbohydrates: 60 g,\nFat: 12 g,\nFiber: 4 g,\nSodium: 1400 mg,\nSugar: 12 g",
  "sourceName": "ten-containers",
